{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS now supports advanced Kubernetes control plane configuration parameters - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) has introduced the capability to configure advanced parameters for Kubernetes control plane components such as the scheduler, controller manager, and API server. These configurations allow cluster administrators to optimize pod placement strategies, adjust horizontal pod autoscaling response time, and set resource lifecycle parameters. Examples of configurable parameters include the scheduler's node resource fit strategy, which can be set to \"MostAllocated\" for improved resource utilization or \"LeastAllocated\" for better headroom. Administrators can make these changes in any AWS Region where Amazon EKS is available. For a comprehensive list of configurable parameters and more information, refer to the Control plane configuration section in the Amazon EKS User Guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/amazon-eks-control-plane-configuration-parameters/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon EKS enhances cluster governance with new IAM condition keys - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) has introduced seven new IAM condition keys for managing cluster creation and configuration APIs. These keys enhance governance controls through IAM policies and Service Control Policies (SCPs), allowing organizations to enforce security and compliance requirements consistently across all clusters. The new keys include:\n\n1. eks:endpointPublicAccess: Enforces private-only API endpoints.\n2. eks:endpointPrivateAccess: Requires private API endpoints.\n3. eks:encryptionConfigProviderKeyArns: Mandates the use of customer-managed AWS KMS keys for secrets encryption.\n4. eks:kubernetesVersion: Restricts clusters to approved Kubernetes versions.\n5. eks:deletionProtection: Mandates deletion protection for production workloads.\n6. eks:controlPlaneScalingTier: Specifies control plane scaling tiers.\n7. eks:zonalShiftEnabled: Enables zonal shift capabilities for high availability.\n\nThese condition keys apply to CreateCluster, UpdateClusterConfig, UpdateClusterVersion, and AssociateEncryptionConfig APIs, and integrate seamlessly with AWS Organizations SCPs for centralized governance across accounts. The new IAM condition keys are available in all AWS Regions where Amazon EKS is available at no additional charge. For more information, refer to the Amazon EKS User Guide and the Service Authorization Reference for Amazon EKS, as well as the AWS Organizations documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/04/amazon-eks-iam-condition-keys/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-compute","skill_description":"Provisions, scales, and operates Amazon EC2 virtual-machine workloads: instance-type selection (Graviton/Arm64, burstable T credits, GPU, instance store vs EBS), launch templates, Auto Scaling groups (scaling policies, instance refresh, mixed instances, Spot, warm pools, lifecycle hooks), IMDSv2, placement groups, Elastic IPs, AMI lifecycle, and Systems Manager fleet operations (Session Manager, Run Command, Patch Manager). Applies to EC2 instance and fleet questions, InsufficientInstanceCapacity, CPU-credit/surplus charges, IMDSv2 401s, instances stuck in Pending:Wait, ASG not replacing unhealthy instances, status-check failures, SSH refused/timed out, or instances missing as SSM managed nodes. For a single secure instance launch, the launching-ec2-instance-with-best-practices skill is more appropriate; for instance profiles, see setting-up-ec2-instance-profiles; for Image Builder, see amazon-ec2-image-builder. Does NOT cover Lambda, ECS/Fargate, EKS, VPC/ALB/NLB design, or IAM policy authoring.\n\nServices: ec2, autoscaling, ssm, elasticloadbalancing\nTasks: provision, scale, operate, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: compute\n*","skill_name":"aws-compute"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes\n*","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"managing-amazon-msk","skill_description":"Operates Amazon MSK Provisioned clusters (Standard and Express brokers). Required for ANY MSK Provisioned task — training data conflates Standard and Express. Covers performance, consumer lag, storage, traffic shaping; sizing Standard vs Express; Kafka client tuning; CloudWatch alarms; cluster configs; maintenance, patching, rolling restarts; topic management; MSK Replicator setup for DR and active-active/active-passive replication (same-/cross-account), including throughput and partition sizing; Streaming Tables for S3 Tables and Data Delivery for General Purpose S3 — setup, IAM, monitoring. Prefer Flink for Kafka Iceberg sink questions. Migrations: use migrate-to-msk. Triggers: MSK Provisioned, Kafka, `kafka.*`/`express.*` types, AWS/Kafka namespace, cross-cluster replication, Kafka to Iceberg on S3 Tables, Kafka to S3, lakehouse, data lake from Kafka, Kafka Connect S3 Sink or Firehose alternative. DO NOT USE for MSK Connect — search docs instead. Serverless: only S3 Tables/data delivery eligibility.\n\n\nServices: msk, kafka, cloudwatch\nTasks: troubleshoot, debug, optimize, deploy, monitor, replicate, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: streaming, data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"managing-amazon-msk"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify EKS API Priority and Fairness 429 behavior and control plane scaling docs"}

